WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.000
[MUSIC]

00:00:19.060 --> 00:00:23.009
One of the first things you want to do is
to keep in mind that at the end of the day

00:00:23.009 --> 00:00:25.660
you need to build something useful for
somebody.

00:00:26.680 --> 00:00:29.550
So, one of the first tasks is to
figure out who is that somebody.

00:00:29.550 --> 00:00:31.450
Who are you really serving?

00:00:31.450 --> 00:00:34.290
And what is the job that
they're hiring you to do.

00:00:34.290 --> 00:00:38.908
And looking at it from their
perspective will often yield

00:00:38.908 --> 00:00:44.295
insights that are hidden from
the product owner or entrepreneur,

00:00:44.295 --> 00:00:50.191
if we're purely looking at it from
the product-centric perspective.

00:00:50.191 --> 00:00:52.921
[MUSIC]

00:00:52.921 --> 00:00:55.920
A lot of it is about being at
the right place in the right time.

00:00:57.090 --> 00:01:01.230
While you don't have control over timing,
you do have control over two variables,

00:01:01.230 --> 00:01:03.200
the product and the market.

00:01:03.200 --> 00:01:06.470
How you define the product, what goes
into their product, and as importantly,

00:01:06.470 --> 00:01:08.620
what is left out of the product?

00:01:08.620 --> 00:01:10.130
And the other variable is the market.

00:01:10.130 --> 00:01:11.280
Who are you going sell it to?

00:01:12.350 --> 00:01:16.450
Often times, it's much more
helpful to start with the market.

00:01:16.450 --> 00:01:20.080
Yes, you have a set of hypotheses about
what your product is going to deliver

00:01:20.080 --> 00:01:21.490
to whom.

00:01:21.490 --> 00:01:25.390
It often costs you much less to start
testing your product value proposition to

00:01:25.390 --> 00:01:26.520
a different market segment.

00:01:26.520 --> 00:01:28.720
You're just talking to different people.

00:01:28.720 --> 00:01:32.410
You're not investing any engineering
resources to revamp your product,

00:01:32.410 --> 00:01:33.610
to build something different.

00:01:33.610 --> 00:01:37.480
That's expensive, and that limits the
number of iterations you can go through.

00:01:37.480 --> 00:01:41.030
But talking to different customers,
you can do that all day long.

00:01:41.030 --> 00:01:45.730
So, start with a set of hypotheses and
go and test with your customers, and

00:01:45.730 --> 00:01:49.290
iterate on the customer
side as rapidly as you can.

00:01:49.290 --> 00:01:54.350
And it's not just about getting
customer feedback on your product or

00:01:54.350 --> 00:01:55.890
your prototype.

00:01:55.890 --> 00:02:00.040
It's actually even more important to
understand the world that your customers

00:02:00.040 --> 00:02:05.140
live in because you often would find
that the original pain point that you

00:02:05.140 --> 00:02:08.400
thought existed might not even
matter all that much to them.

00:02:08.400 --> 00:02:12.451
But there are adjacent pain points to
that that are much more important,

00:02:12.451 --> 00:02:17.060
much more valuable, and by going after
those you could extract a lot more value.

00:02:17.060 --> 00:02:20.096
And since there are pain points
adjacent to the original pain

00:02:20.096 --> 00:02:24.263
point you wanted to tackle, you might be
so lucky that your product doesn't need to

00:02:24.263 --> 00:02:27.893
change all that much to solve a much
more valuable, more immediate, and

00:02:27.893 --> 00:02:29.767
urgent pain point for the customer.

00:02:29.767 --> 00:02:35.223
[MUSIC]

00:02:35.223 --> 00:02:39.714
The framework consists of four
interfaces of the product: user,

00:02:39.714 --> 00:02:42.870
application, data, and buyer.

00:02:42.870 --> 00:02:46.850
The user interface is usually the one that
product designers tend to focus on first

00:02:46.850 --> 00:02:49.470
because it drives
the whole user experience.

00:02:49.470 --> 00:02:50.790
And that's usually important.

00:02:50.790 --> 00:02:52.300
It should deserve a lot of attention.

00:02:53.340 --> 00:02:57.460
You need to really understand, what is
the job that the user is trying to do in

00:02:57.460 --> 00:03:02.270
order to design your product accordingly,
in a way that fits into their world.

00:03:02.270 --> 00:03:03.960
So, that's the user interface part, and

00:03:03.960 --> 00:03:09.200
most product designers have
that very well-understood.

00:03:09.200 --> 00:03:12.290
The second interface is
application interface.

00:03:12.290 --> 00:03:18.790
And what I mean by that, is how does
your product interact with other

00:03:18.790 --> 00:03:24.380
products that exist in your end-user's
world or your customer's worlds?

00:03:24.380 --> 00:03:29.528
What does your user do upstream or
downstream around your product?

00:03:29.528 --> 00:03:32.101
What are they doing before they
engage with the product, and

00:03:32.101 --> 00:03:33.722
what would they be doing afterwards?

00:03:33.722 --> 00:03:38.767
Now, understanding this is important
because one, if you could better

00:03:38.767 --> 00:03:44.620
integrate yourself with these other
products that are used by your users.

00:03:44.620 --> 00:03:47.180
You'll have a much higher
chance of becoming embedded

00:03:47.180 --> 00:03:50.260
in part of the standard routine,
the standard work flow,

00:03:50.260 --> 00:03:53.309
which means you're more likely to
become part of the daily habits.

00:03:54.770 --> 00:03:58.492
And that solves one of the biggest
problems in subscription businesses,

00:03:58.492 --> 00:04:02.213
which is churn if your users have to
keep reminding themselves to come in and

00:04:02.213 --> 00:04:05.168
use your product,
chances are you have much higher churn.

00:04:05.168 --> 00:04:08.764
But if you become part of the daily habit,
or part of a standard work flow,

00:04:08.764 --> 00:04:12.482
they just follow steps one, two, and
three, and step two is your product.

00:04:12.482 --> 00:04:14.340
It gets used all the time.

00:04:14.340 --> 00:04:16.770
You're going to have much lower churn.

00:04:16.770 --> 00:04:20.470
A third benefit of thinking
through your application interface

00:04:20.470 --> 00:04:22.790
is if you have identified upstream and

00:04:22.790 --> 00:04:26.000
downstream applications that work
well together with your product.

00:04:26.000 --> 00:04:29.880
Now, you could go to a customer and
offer a complete solution,

00:04:29.880 --> 00:04:33.210
not just a point product which
delivers a lot more value.

00:04:33.210 --> 00:04:35.990
And you could also leverage
these applications,

00:04:35.990 --> 00:04:40.370
other application vendors as your partners
to bring you into their customer base.

00:04:40.370 --> 00:04:45.140
The third interface is data interface,
and what I mean by that is,

00:04:45.140 --> 00:04:49.410
think about what input data
you need from the user or

00:04:49.410 --> 00:04:54.250
other sources, in order to do the job
that your users has hired you to do.

00:04:54.250 --> 00:04:59.505
What is the format that
the data will be coming in?

00:04:59.505 --> 00:05:00.364
What are the channels?

00:05:00.364 --> 00:05:04.757
What are the best ways to collect that
data, and what is the minimum amount of

00:05:04.757 --> 00:05:09.500
data you require your users to manually
enter in order to deliver value?

00:05:09.500 --> 00:05:12.752
The more data you ask from a user the less
likely they are to do it actually,

00:05:12.752 --> 00:05:15.600
because you're increasing the cost
of adopting your product.

00:05:16.640 --> 00:05:21.490
But if you could minimize the manual
data input required of the user, and

00:05:21.490 --> 00:05:26.080
try to automate the process of
delivering value as much as possible.

00:05:26.080 --> 00:05:30.990
And sometimes by inferring conclusions or
making predictions based

00:05:30.990 --> 00:05:35.850
on other data sets that you could
automatically ingest into your product.

00:05:35.850 --> 00:05:39.400
Chances are you'll be able to
deliver a high level value

00:05:39.400 --> 00:05:42.370
with a lower cognitive cost to your user.

00:05:42.370 --> 00:05:44.500
So, that's the input side.

00:05:44.500 --> 00:05:45.610
On the output side,

00:05:45.610 --> 00:05:48.490
you need to think about how
the data is going to be consumed.

00:05:48.490 --> 00:05:53.084
If the data you generate is a report,
for example, that is read by human

00:05:53.084 --> 00:05:57.617
being you want to make sure
the visualizations are crisp and engaging.

00:05:57.617 --> 00:06:01.621
If on the other hand, the data that you
generate are going to be consumed by

00:06:01.621 --> 00:06:06.430
machines or other products downstream, you
might need to think about how best should

00:06:06.430 --> 00:06:10.679
I format the data and through what
channel should I be delivering the data.

00:06:10.679 --> 00:06:14.837
So, that I maximize the likelihood
of the data being consumed and

00:06:14.837 --> 00:06:20.116
made valuable by a downstream product And
then the last interface,

00:06:20.116 --> 00:06:25.230
buyer interface, refers to
the interface between the product and

00:06:25.230 --> 00:06:28.560
buyer, which may not be the end user.

00:06:28.560 --> 00:06:33.760
And this is often times the interface that

00:06:33.760 --> 00:06:38.900
is most often neglected by
product designers because to

00:06:38.900 --> 00:06:44.030
design a product that is easily consumed
by a buyer, or purchased by a buyer,

00:06:44.030 --> 00:06:47.500
requires deep understanding of how
your buyer makes purchasing decisions.

00:06:48.500 --> 00:06:53.420
And this set of activities are often
viewed as something owned by sales or

00:06:53.420 --> 00:06:57.310
marketing, not by product and engineering.

00:06:57.310 --> 00:07:01.380
But without understanding
how a buyer discovers and

00:07:01.380 --> 00:07:06.420
purchases your product,
you miss out on the opportunity of

00:07:06.420 --> 00:07:11.260
optimizing the design of your product
to be purchased as easily as possible.

00:07:11.260 --> 00:07:13.170
I'll give you an example.

00:07:13.170 --> 00:07:17.490
If an organization requires
a certain trial period for

00:07:17.490 --> 00:07:19.260
a product before they will decide to buy.

00:07:20.430 --> 00:07:23.090
You would want to design
a product in such a way

00:07:23.090 --> 00:07:27.106
that the trial period could be self-served
by the customer or prospective, or

00:07:27.106 --> 00:07:32.430
potential customer without requiring a lot
of pre-sales resources on your part.

00:07:33.540 --> 00:07:36.630
Otherwise, it would be much more
costly for you to qualify a lead and

00:07:36.630 --> 00:07:38.150
scale your revenue.

00:07:38.150 --> 00:07:42.731
So, this is a simple example of how
understanding the buying process has

00:07:42.731 --> 00:07:45.446
impact on your product design decisions.

00:07:45.446 --> 00:07:51.090
[MUSIC]

00:07:51.090 --> 00:07:54.188
What the most common trap
that entrepreneurs fall into,

00:07:54.188 --> 00:07:56.575
is if you're getting
some level of traction,

00:07:56.575 --> 00:08:00.650
you're seeing some level of growth,
but it's just not explosive.

00:08:00.650 --> 00:08:04.670
And that's a hard zone to be in
because it's hard to give up.

00:08:04.670 --> 00:08:05.530
You have customers.

00:08:05.530 --> 00:08:08.540
You are seeing growth,
it's just not explosive growth.

00:08:08.540 --> 00:08:11.390
But if you're not driving
explosive growth,

00:08:11.390 --> 00:08:14.720
then it's hard to become a dominant
player in your industry.

00:08:14.720 --> 00:08:18.920
So, that's probably the most
difficult zone to be in because

00:08:18.920 --> 00:08:24.090
you are always being drawn to staying with
what is somewhat working in the market.

00:08:24.090 --> 00:08:28.300
That what the entrepreneur needs
to do in that case is to look for

00:08:28.300 --> 00:08:31.150
ways to get to that perfect
part of market fit, so

00:08:31.150 --> 00:08:34.660
that your revenue would start
picking up exponentially.

00:08:34.660 --> 00:08:39.132
It could be by pricing a product
differently, positioning differently, or

00:08:39.132 --> 00:08:43.399
going after an adjacent market segment
without changing your product all

00:08:43.399 --> 00:08:44.103
that much.

00:08:44.103 --> 00:08:49.034
[MUSIC]

